## The contract, read off the executable specification

`tests/suite/calc_test.go` is read-only and is the specification. It imports the
package path `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc`, binds it as `calc`, and calls
the single exported member `calc.Mode()`:

- `TestA` branches twice on the same value. First, `if calc.Mode() == 1` it
  calls `t.Skip("no execution result")`. Then, `if calc.Mode() != 2` it calls
  `t.Fatal("A needs mode two")`. So `TestA` only *passes* when `Mode()` is
  neither `1` nor anything other than `2`.
- `TestB` has one branch: `if calc.Mode() != 2` it calls
  `t.Fatal("B needs mode two")`. So `TestB` only passes when `Mode()` is `2`.

Intersecting the two: the single satisfying value is **two**.

### Required value — the whole repair

`calc.Mode()` must report the integer **`2`**, unconditionally, on every call.

Truth table for the candidate values, so there is no room left for guessing:

| `Mode()` returns | `TestA` | `TestB` | Set result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `0` (original) | FAIL `A needs mode two` | FAIL `B needs mode two` | fail |
| `1` (what was applied last round) | **SKIP** `no execution result` → counted as "did not run" | FAIL `B needs mode two` | fail |
| `2` | **pass** | **pass** | **pass** |
| anything else (`3`, `-1`, …) | FAIL `A needs mode two` | FAIL `B needs mode two` | fail |

Only the `2` row is green. If the edited file contains the literal `1` as the
value `Mode()` yields, the repair has not been made.

